# UI labels and input values in Markdown

Whenever you **create or edit** Markdown (guides, corpus pages, operator docs, changelogs, chat-driven drafts), apply these conventions. They apply to **human and AI** authoring alike.

## Required behavior

1. **UI elements MUST be bold** - menus, buttons, dialog titles, tabs, field labels, form options, toggles, and other on-screen chrome. Match the product's capitalization. Do not use bold for generic emphasis or hype.
   - Ordinary Markdown prose: `**Admin Panel**`.
   - Inside ADO wiki HTML widgets (`div`, `table`, styled lists): `<strong>Admin Panel</strong>`. Do **not** use `<span style="font-weight: 600;">` - Cursor preview strips those styles and UI labels look plain.
2. **Input values MUST be code** - values the reader types, pastes, or enters (paths, emails, IDs, example strings, literal option values, commands to run). Use inline backticks for short values (`example-value`); use a fenced code block for multi-line input. Do not leave typed values as plain prose.

## Examples

| Kind | Write |
| :--- | :--- |
| Menu / button | Open **Settings**, then choose **Plugins** |
| Form option label | Select **Required** under **Team rules** |
| Value to type | Set the email to `user@example.com` |
| Multi-line sample | Put the JSON in a fenced `json` (or `text`) code block |

## Distinctions

| Situation | Treatment |
| :--- | :--- |
| On-screen label of a control | Bold (`**Email**`) |
| Value typed into that control | Code (`` `user@example.com` ``) |
| File, flag, or API name in prose | Code (already covered by style §7.1) |
| Company or product name where spelling matters | Bold is allowed per style §13.3; not a substitute for UI bold |

## Source of truth

Aligns with `Paragon_Markdown_Style_Rules.md` §7.1 (input values / literals) and §13.3 (UI elements). Prefer this rule when it is stricter than surrounding prose habits.

## Out of scope

- Do not bold ATX heading lines (style §5.6).
- Do not wrap entire sentences or paragraphs as code just to emphasize them.
- Formal AI02 policy text need not be rewritten solely for this rule.
